  "query_preview": "..\n(Today through next Thursday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: Upper level closed low continues to \nprogress east and into the intermountain west today while\nconcurrently weakening, opening into a deep upper level trof. The\ntrof axis is still projected to be situated over Nevada by \ntonight after midnight. Moisture continues to be advected into \nthe Great Basin today from the south. Rain showers and \nthunderstorms will become more prevalent as the morning \nprogresses and activity increases from the southwest in \nconjunction with the upper level trof moving into the \nintermountain west, providing additional dynamical forcing to aid \nin shower and thunderstorm production. Showers and thunderstorms \ncontinue thru most of tonight. A few thunderstorms may become \nsevere this afternoon and evening, primarily across the eastern \nhalf of the forecast area. The Storm Prediction Center has issued\na marginal risk of severe thunderstorms including northeast Nye \nand White Pine counties, as well as portions of Elko County. \nShowers will continue through the day Friday, though activity will\ndissipate from west to east as the upper level trof continues to \nprogress east into Utah. Snow levels lower from around 10,000 feet\nto around 6,000 to 7,000 feet early Friday morning as cooler air \nmoves into Nevada from the northwest. Significant valley snow \naccumulation is not expected and a few locales along the Idaho and\nOregon borders may see less than an inch of accumulation. Storm \ntotal liquid equivalent precipitation accumulation for the four \nday period that began Tuesday and runs thru Friday is still \nbetween one quarter and three quarters of an inch. The high \nelevations look to reach one inch or more, much of which will fall\nas snow above 10,000 feet.\n\nThe upper level trof is indicated to split into a closed low as\nreinforcing energy advects south from the PacNW along the upstream\nside of the trof. The closed low will form over SoCal and western\nArizona by Saturday morning while the remainder of the trof \nitself progresses to the northeast and into Montana. An upper \nlevel ridge and high pressure look to reside offshore of the west \ncoast. All that to say weather will be generally fair beginning \nSaturday and thru the middle of next week. There is potential for\nlight precipitation in the Monday/Monday night timeframe as a \nweak cold front moves thru the PacNW and clips northern Nevada, \nthough based on the current modeled trajectory of this weak \nsystem, will be maintaining dry condtions in the forecast at this\ntime. \n\nFriday and Saturday nights will be the coldest of the forecast \nperiod, with widespread lows in the 20s across the area. Mid to \nupper teens can be expected in the typical colder valleys, \nespecially Saturday night under clearer skies and light to calm \nwinds. A modest warming trend will be present thru the weekend and\ninto Monday, though temperatures remain cool and more seasonal \nnext week.\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: There is high confidence for\nshowers and thunderstorms today and tomorrow with the majority of\nthe activity occurring today.\n\nThere is high confidence in the return of fair weather Saturday\nthough moderate confidence in the potential for precipitaton\nalong the OR/ID borders on Monday/Monday night. \n\nSignificant edits required within PoP and weather fields to\nproduce a more accurate representation of forecast precipitaton\nthru Friday.",
